Cenotaph Swing

Ingredients: COOL & WEST COAST JAZZ + ELECTRO SWING + DARK, SINISTER & GOTHIC + Arpeggiator
Creative Catalyst: Reverse-engineered from a heat-warped magnetic cassette unsealed from a 1959 municipal time capsule in foggy Monterey.

Cenotaph Swing is the sound of mid-century Pacific jazz dragged through a haunted dancehall. It grafts the dry, laconic counterpoint of West Coast cool jazz—bleached baritone saxophones, subdued Harmon-muted trumpets, and effortless brushed rides—onto the relentless four-on-the-floor swing-house drive of electro swing. Tying this combustible marriage together is a cold, gothic underbelly: menacing minor-mode arrangements, churchyard pipe organs, and relentless 16th-note analog synthesizer arpeggios that tick like doomed clockwork behind smoky brass solos.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 114–122 BPM. A rigid, syncopated four-on-the-floor house kick layered with loping Lindy-hop swing shuffles, locked tightly to sixteenth-note synth arpeggiations.
- Harmonic Palette: Dorian and Aeolian modal frameworks, chromatic passing tones, melancholic minor ninths, and unresolved tritone suspensions played with church-crypt solemnity.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Tape-Flutter Sidechaining: Subdued horn duets violently ducked against aggressive swing kicks and modular arpeggiator gates, mimicking damaged magnetic tape.
  2. Gothic Space Contrast: Muted, intimate solo brass recorded completely dry in small dead rooms, contrasted against cavernous, sepulchral plate reverbs on chopped big-band vocal snippets.
  3. Rotary Wow Modulation: Pitch-warped gramophone crackle layered beneath crisp modern transients, giving the swing chops an uneasy, decomposing weight.
- Dynamics: Songs open with sparse, breathy horn whispers and hypnotic, icy arpeggiator pulses before snapping abruptly into punishing swing grooves, collapsing periodically into suffocating, sub-bass-heavy breakdowns.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the damp, salt-corroded basements of Pacific Grove and Seattle, the Cenotaph Swing movement sprouted when crate-digging synth programmers attempted to restore an unlabeled 1959 civic time-capsule tape. What emerged from the magnetic rot sounded like an impossible anachronism: a West Coast jazz quintet playing alongside a ticking mechanical metronome that seemed to predict electronic dance music. A cultish warehouse subculture followed. Dressed in tailored charcoal-grey zoot suits, lace mourning veils, and silver lapel stopwatches intentionally frozen at 4:17 AM, scene acolytes gather for "The Escapement Rites." Dancers perform high-speed, clipped Lindy-hop footwork while maintaining rigid, stone-faced upper bodies, moving through clouds of clove smoke and dry ice.

Typical Instruments: Harmon-muted trumpet, baritone saxophone, upright bass doubled with sub-bass synthesizers, Roland System-100 analog arpeggiator, 1930s big-band gramophone samples, brushed snare loops, and gothic pipe organ pedals.

